CREATE AN INDEMNITY FOR MATTERS UNRELATED TO YOUR ACTIVITIES. "THIRD PARTY"
EXCLUDES THE PROTECTED PARTY SEEKING RECOVERY AND YOU; YOUR OWN DIRECT CLAIM
IS GOVERNED BY SECTION 13, NOT CONVERTED INTO AN INDEMNITY CLAIM UNDER THIS
SECTION. REGULATORY DEFENSE COSTS OR CIVIL AMOUNTS ARE COVERED ONLY WHERE
LAWFULLY SHIFTABLE; CRIMINAL FINES AND NON-INDEMNIFIABLE PENALTIES ARE NOT.

14.2 CLAIM PROCEDURE. The Protected Party must notify You reasonably promptly
after learning of a potentially covered claim; delay relieves You only to the
extent it materially prejudices the defense. Upon written tender of a claim
alleging facts within Section 14.1 and not wholly excluded by Section 14.3,
You must promptly assume its defense through competent counsel reasonably
acceptable to the Protected Party and pay reasonable covered defense costs as
incurred. If applicable law prohibits an advance defense obligation, You must
instead reimburse covered costs when legally payable. For mixed claims,
allocate separable covered costs reasonably; reasonably inseparable costs
needed to defend the covered allegations are advanced, subject to adjustment
under Section 14.3. A bare, unsupported demand is not conclusive evidence of
coverage or the amount payable.

The Protected Party must reasonably cooperate, at Your reasonable expense,
without surrendering privilege or breaching law. It may participate through
its own counsel at its own expense, except You pay reasonable separate-counsel
costs when an actual material conflict or applicable law requires separate
representation. If You fail promptly to provide an adequate defense, it may
defend itself and recover reasonable covered costs. A Protected Party may
control a regulatory defense where law requires or the authority so directs;
this does not expand what costs are legally indemnifiable.

You may not settle a claim without the Protected Party's prior written consent
if the settlement lacks a complete release for it, admits its fault, requires
it to pay, or imposes an injunction or other nonmonetary obligation on it.
Consent must not be unreasonably withheld for a settlement fully funded by You
that provides an unconditional release and imposes none of those burdens.
A Protected Party must obtain Your consent, not unreasonably withheld, before
settling at Your expense, unless You wrongfully refuse or abandon the covered
defense; in that event only a reasonable, good-faith covered settlement is
recoverable. Both sides must take reasonable steps to mitigate covered loss.

14.3 EXCLUSIONS AND ALLOCATION. No duty arises to the extent a loss is caused
by the Protected Party's fraud, gross negligence, willful misconduct, material
breach of its own express obligations, or conduct for which law prohibits
indemnification. No duty arises for a claim based solely on an inherent defect
or pre-existing infringement in unmodified Covered Material as supplied by
that Protected Party, independent of Your acts or omissions contributing to
the claim. Where mandatory consumer law applies to You, indemnification is
limited to third-party loss caused by Your material breach, unlawful conduct
or negligent or intentional misconduct; it does not shift the Protected
Party's own negligence or statutory responsibilities to You.

If a claim is partly excluded, indemnification applies only to the covered
portion. A final adjudication, agreed allocation or lawful settlement governs
that allocation; the Protected Party must return advanced amounts ultimately
determined not to be covered. Nothing requires waiver of a public body's
nonwaivable immunity, funding limits or contracting restrictions. No party
receives double recovery for the same loss.

14.4 Each Protected Party is an intended beneficiary of Sections 12 through
14, entitled to invoke them to the extent applicable law permits. No software
or AI system is a beneficiary. Section 13 caps liability OF Protected Parties;
it does not cap indemnity owed TO them under this Section. No contractual cap
on that indemnity is supplied, but mandatory law and Section 14.3 always apply.
Fees for a direct action to enforce this License or indemnity are recoverable
only to the extent separately authorized by applicable law, not merely because
third-party defense fees are covered here. Accrued coverage survives expiration
or termination. This promise is not insurance or a guarantee of collection.

